# Deployment Strategies

One of the most important concepts in CodeDeploy is the deployment strategy.

Two major strategies are:

## 1. In-Place Deployment

In an in-place deployment, the existing application is stopped and the new version is deployed to the same instances.

Example:

```text
Old Version
     ↓
Existing EC2
     ↓
Stop Application
     ↓
Deploy New Version
     ↓
Start Application
     ↓
New Version
```

Advantages:

- Simple
- Cost-effective
- Does not require duplicate infrastructure

Disadvantage:

- There can be downtime depending on the deployment configuration.

---

# 2. Blue/Green Deployment

In a blue/green deployment, the new application version is deployed to a separate environment before traffic is shifted to it.

```text
                 ┌──────────────┐
                 │    Users     │
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                     Traffic
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐
                 │ Load Balancer│
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
             ┌──────────┴──────────┐
             ↓                     ↓
        Blue Environment      Green Environment
         Old Version            New Version
```

Once the new version is tested successfully, traffic can be shifted to the new environment.

If something goes wrong, traffic can be shifted back to the previous version.

### Advantages

- Reduced downtime
- Safer deployments
- Easier rollback
- Useful for production environments

### Disadvantage

It can require additional infrastructure, which may increase cost.

---

# CodeDeploy Deployment Configuration

CodeDeploy provides different deployment configurations that control how many instances receive the new version at a time.

For example:

```text
AllAtOnce
HalfAtATime
OneAtATime
```

### AllAtOnce

Deploys the new version to all targeted instances at the same time.

```text
EC2-1 ── New Version
EC2-2 ── New Version
EC2-3 ── New Version
```

This is fast but can have greater availability risk.

### OneAtATime

Deploys to one instance at a time.

```text
EC2-1 → New Version
EC2-2 → New Version
EC2-3 → New Version
```

This can provide better availability but takes longer.

---

# Rollback

CodeDeploy can help recover from unsuccessful deployments.

For example:

```text
Version 1
    ↓
Deploy Version 2
    ↓
Deployment Failure
    ↓
Rollback
    ↓
Version 1
```

Rollback is important because it allows teams to quickly return to a known working version.

The exact rollback behavior depends on the deployment configuration and how the deployment is set up.

